{
  "teamId": 135,
  "year": 2018,
  "narrative": "The 2018 San Diego Padres were a franchise in transition, caught between a rebuilding present and an uncertain future. They opened the year with a 0-3 March and never truly found their footing, finishing a distant 66-96 — one of the worst records in the National League. Yet the season was not without its bright spots, and it told the story of what the Padres might one day become.\n\nThe early months offered a few tantalizing glimpses of life. Hunter Renfroe emerged as the team's most explosive offensive weapon, delivering walk-off moments and clutch blasts throughout spring. Eric Hosmer, the prized free-agent addition, contributed his share of big hits, and Freddy Galvis provided steady veteran production. A surprising May (15-13) briefly raised hopes that the Padres could tread water in the NL West, but those hopes were fragile.\n\nJuly shattered whatever optimism remained. The Padres went a catastrophic 5-20, and Brad Hand — who had been the anchor of the bullpen — was traded away at the deadline, a signal that the front office had shifted to full teardown mode. The high-leverage plays from that stretch told a brutal story: Tyson Ross surrendering a grand slam, Hand allowing key runs before his departure, the offense going cold when it mattered most. It was the moment the season officially died.\n\nThe final two months offered small consolations. Franmil Reyes announced his arrival with a towering home run. Renfroe finished strong, slugging his 26th homer on the penultimate day of the season. Freddy Galvis doubled home the go-ahead run in mid-September. These were the seeds of something — young players gaining experience, building blocks being identified — but in 2018, the Padres were simply not ready. The 96 losses were a reminder of how far San Diego still had to go before returning to contention.",
